Subject: wasix: refuse to unlink a directory before removing its entry
Last-Update: 2026-09-30

path_unlink_file removed the entry from the parent directory and
decremented the link count before it looked at what it had removed, and
only then answered EISDIR for a directory. The directory stayed in
place, in the memory filesystem or on the host, but the parent no
longer listed it until a later lookup recreated the entry. Go's
os.Remove unlinks first and rmdirs on EISDIR, so it failed with ENOENT
and left the directory; os.RemoveAll took the ENOENT of its next step
as already gone and returned success with the tree intact. The check
now comes first, as unlink(2) does, and nothing is touched.

diff --git a/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/wasi/path_unlink_file.rs b/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/wasi/path_unlink_file.rs
index 83844c4..0d87b5f 100644
--- a/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/wasi/path_unlink_file.rs
+++ b/lib/wasix/src/syscalls/wasi/path_unlink_file.rs
@@ -56,6 +56,12 @@ pub(crate) fn path_unlink_file_internal(
     let (memory, mut state, inodes) = unsafe { env.get_memory_and_wasi_state_and_inodes(&ctx, 0) };
 
     let inode = wasi_try_ok!(state.fs.get_inode_at_path(inodes, fd, path, false));
+    // unlink(2) refuses a directory before touching anything. Refusing it
+    // below, after the parent's entry was removed, left the directory in
+    // place but unreachable until a lookup recreated the entry.
+    if matches!(inode.read().deref(), Kind::Dir { .. } | Kind::Root { .. }) {
+        return Ok(Errno::Isdir);
+    }
     let (parent_inode, child_name) = wasi_try_ok!(state.fs.get_parent_inode_at_path(
         inodes,
         fd,
